288:\section{Introduction}
290:It is well-known that low-rank approximations of matrices are well-behaved: 
291:For every matrix, there is a best low-rank approximation with a fixed error, 
292:and every element closer to the original matrix must be of larger rank. 
293:In other words, the approximate rank
295:\rank^{\varepsilon}(T) \coloneqq \inf_{W \in B_{\varepsilon}(T)} \rank(W)
297:where $B_{\varepsilon}(T)$ is the $\varepsilon$-ball around an element $T$ for a given norm $\Vert \cdot \Vert$, coincides with the exact rank when $\varepsilon > 0$ is small enough.
