640:\subsection{Decomposing multipartite psd matrices}
641:\label{ssec:borderRanksMatrixDecompositions}
643:The three types of positive decompositions for nonnegative tensors are related to the three positive decompositions for multipartite psd matrices (see \Cref{ssec:matrixDec}). This enables us to translate gaps between border ranks and ranks for positive tensor decompositions to gaps between border rank and rank for multipartite psd matrices. Given a tensor $T$, such that $\bpsdrank(T) < \psdrank(T)$, the matrix $\rho_T$ (Equation \eqref{eq:rhoT}) satisfies
645:\bpurirank(\rho_T) &\leq  \bpsdrank(T)\\
646:&< \psdrank(T) \\ &= \purirank(\rho_T).
648:and thereby exhibits a gap between border rank and rank for $\purirank$. Analogously, one obtains gaps for matrix tensor decompositions whenever there is a gap in the corresponding tensor decomposition. This strategy results in gaps between border rank and rank for $\symmpurirank$, $\tipuriosr$, $\seposr$, and $\tiseposr$.
650:\subsection{Non-existence of border rank gaps}
653:In the following, we provide two cases where no gaps between border rank and rank appear. First, we establish that for standard tensor decompositions (i.e.\ only containing one summation index), the $\nnrank$, $\symmnnrank$, $\seprank$, and the $\symmseprank$ do not exhibit a gap. Second, we prove that $\Omega$-decompositions arising from a tree $\Omega$ do not exhibit gaps between rank and border rank regardless of the positivity constraints.
655:The proof strategy is similar in both cases. We first show that every decomposition can be reduced to a particular normalized version (i.e.\ every local element satisfies a normalization constraint). Then, we apply the Bolzano--Weierstraß Theorem to the local elements to guarantee that every sequence of decompositions obtained from a converging sequence of global elements converges to a decomposition of the same rank.
657:\subsubsection{Nonnegative and separable standard tensor decompositions}
659:Let us now show that $\nnrank$, $\symmnnrank$, $\seprank$, and the $\symmseprank$ do not exhibit a gap between rank and border rank.
