719:\section{Consequences}
722:Let us now present two implications of the existence of gaps between rank and border rank in multipartite positive tensor decompositions. 
723:In \Cref{ssec:rankCorrelation} we show a correspondence between positive tensor decompositions and quantum correlation sets. The gaps between border ranks and ranks then imply that certain sets of quantum correlations are not closed.
724:In \Cref{ssec:borderSeparation} we prove that gaps also lead to new types of separations between positive tensor ranks.
728:\caption{\textbf{Positive ranks characterize correlation scenarios.} (a) For a nonnegative tensor $P$ representing an $n$-partite probability distribution, the standard nonnegative rank characterizes the range of a shared hidden variable to generate $P$ via local conditional probability distributions. (b) $\psdrank_{\Omega}(P)$ characterizes the minimal $\rank_{\Omega}$ of a resource state $\ket{\psi}$ to generate $P$ via local measurements. (c) For an $n$-partite density matrix $\rho$, $\purirank_{\Omega}(\rho)$ characterizes the minimal $\rank_{\Omega}$ of a resource state $\ket{\psi}$ to generate $\rho$ via local quantum channels.}
732:\subsection{Sets of quantum correlations are not closed}
733:\label{ssec:rankCorrelation}
735:Positive tensor decompositions give rise to statements about (quantum) correlations. Consider a bipartite finite probability distribution represented as an entrywise nonnegative tensor $P \in \mathbb{C}^d \otimes \mathbb{C}^d$ via $$P_{ij} = P(X = i, Y =j)$$ where $X$ and $Y$ are random values taking values in $\{1,\ldots, d\}$. It is known that the non-negative rank of $P$ corresponds to the minimal range of a hidden variable $\Lambda$ necessary to sample this distribution locally. Similarly, the $\psdrank(P)$ is the minimal Schmidt rank of the bipartite state $\ket{\psi}$ necessary to generate $P$ via local measurements on each qudit \cite{Fa14, Ja13}.
738:We now show that these correspondences generalize to multipartite tensor decompositions as well as multipartite matrix tensor decompositions. Together with the existence of non-trivial border ranks, this entails that these sets of probability distributions as well as density matrices are topologically not closed.
740:\subsubsection{Entanglement in correlation scenarios and positive ranks}
